return await window.__createIslands([{"id":"ess-24","kind":"prose","x":3200,"y":1400,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>A transmitter sends carrier waves through an exhibition space. A receiver waits, tuned and retuned, sometimes coinciding with the transmitted frequency, sometimes missing it. At unpredictable moments, a signal becomes audible. Then it withdraws again into the continuum of activity that was always present but not available to hearing. The work is not the sound alone. It is the relation between transmission and audibility, between active field and brief perceptual aperture, between the technical fact of emission and the aesthetic event of reception. This relation is crucial because it resists the reduction of art to experience. The work continues even when it is not heard. It radiates without guarantee of apprehension. Its silence is not emptiness but missed reception, or reception deferred, or reception occurring outside the body. The receiver does not simply reveal the work; it participates in making the work intermittently available. In this way, the piece produces an acoustic phenomenology of absence without becoming metaphorical. It asks the listener to attend to what exceeds listening, to imagine the gallery as crossed by organised energies whose perceptual arrival depends on tuning, timing, and coincidence.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-25","kind":"prose","x":3800,"y":1400,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>There is a precise beauty in the moment when transmitter and receiver meet. It is not the beauty of expression, nor the beauty of form in the classical sense, but the beauty of conjugation: a technical and temporal alignment through which an otherwise inaccessible event enters the sensorium. For a moment, frequency, apparatus, space, and listener coincide. The sound appears as if from nowhere, although it has not come from nowhere. It has come from a field of prior activity whose presence was real but unavailable. Then the alignment breaks. The sound disappears, though the conditions of its disappearance are as significant as the conditions of its emergence. Such moments teach a different model of aesthetic experience. Experience is not continuous possession. It is intermittent access. The artwork is not identical with what the viewer hears, sees, or understands, but with the larger operational structure from which those moments are drawn. One might say that the work becomes perceptible only in fragments, but this would still privilege perception too much. More accurately, perception becomes one fragment of the work.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-26","kind":"prose","x":4400,"y":1400,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>A carrier wave may carry information, music, voice, data, noise, or command. But what happens when the signal begins to fold back toward the condition of transmission itself? An electromagnetic self-portrait is not a picture of an artist, nor an image of a device, nor even a representation of a wave. It is an arrangement in which the medium indicates its own activity. The portrait is no longer resemblance but modulation. Such a gesture belongs to a lineage of reflexive practices, but it also departs from them because its reflexivity is not primarily visual, linguistic, or institutional. It is energetic and technical. The work does not say \"this is art\" or \"this is a transmitter\" in the manner of Conceptual Art's linguistic propositions. It performs the relation by which transmission becomes available to reception. In doing so, it raises a more general question: can an artwork portray the conditions that allow it to exist? Not by illustrating them, but by making those conditions operational, unstable, and perceptually contingent? The self-portrait becomes less the image of a subject than the temporary exposure of a circuit.</p></div>"}]);